WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:06.720
Why are cities full of uncomfortable benches?

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.930
This one has armrests to prevent you from
dozing off.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.820
to prevent you from taking a nap.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:16.170
Here’s another — again with the arms,
the stiff metal.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.720
And this one — it’s brand new.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.260
The MTA in New York began installing them
as part of a subway enhancement plan.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.320
And they don’t call it a bench.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.220
They prefer the term "leaning bars."

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:34.880
So what if i told you it was designed with
discomfort in mind?

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.340
New York City is filled with some of the most
innovative architecture and urban planning

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.360
in the world.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.570
Today, nearly every kind of public space here
is has been developed with close attention

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.030
to detail.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.530
So these benches are no mistake.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.220
They are designed to allow you to sit but
not get too cozy.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.100
And that is intentional.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.420
The concept stems from a school of thought
that goes by many names, but today

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.890
we'll use "defensive design."

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.080
Defensive design is about moderating behavior.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.470
The goal is to limit the ways an object can
be misused.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:11.310
These benches have armrests because that will
prevent anyone from laying down.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:16.870
Their short back is another nod to say, "This
bench isn’t yours forever."

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:17.890
This trend is worldwide.

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.370
And it’s not just in the benches.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:25.500
When you start looking for defensive designs
in New York City, you’ll find examples everywhere.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:31.250
It’s the presence of security cameras in
subway turnstiles or Times Square.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:34.520
It’s these spikes on this column, meant
to deter birds.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.460
It’s the knobs on these ledges, to discourage
skateboarders.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.310
And there were once sprinklers underneath
the awning of this bookstore, to prevent people

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.520
from sleeping there.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:45.979
It's sidewalk barriers.

00:01:45.979 --> 00:01:48.810
It’s even these regular streetlights.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:52.490
Streetlights are probably some of the most
recognizable defensive designs.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:58.490
When they surfaced in the 19th-century Western
cities, the dynamic of urban life changed.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:02.869
More people spent time outside at night, which
drove economic development and a reduction

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:04.530
in crime.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.430
Most hostile architecture tries to influence
behavior in a similar way.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:13.810
The designs attempt to make public space a
bit more hospitable, more ideal.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.730
Defensive designs can deter crime.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:19.330
It can prevent the destruction of public property.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.410
And it can prevent loitering.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.580
But there is a reason why defensive design
is characterized as “hostile.”

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:30.210
Take the example of the bench again.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.560
Disability advocates have a problem with that
appearing in the MTA.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:38.350
One advocate pointed out that "People who
travel who have disabilities or just get tired

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:41.890
sometimes need a bench to sit on, not a wall
to lean against."

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:45.450
And while no one likes an uncomfortable bench,

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.510
these additions mean something more for people who are
experiencing homelessness.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:57.239
The United States is currently experiencing
a decline in the overall homeless population.

00:02:57.239 --> 00:03:01.540
But in New York, the homeless population is
growing.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:06.200
About 1800 people were found to have been
sleeping in the subway.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:13.330
That’s because emergency shelter isn’t
always a viable option.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:18.020
There are several examples of hostile architecture
that target people who are homeless.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:22.260
These designs imply that public space is not
where homeless people should be.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:29.080
As it goes, city planners have a dilemma — how
do they design inclusive cities?

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.620
As for the enhanced subway initiative, the
MTA’s mock designs highlight new USB ports

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.240
and electronic signage in stations.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.519
But you won’t find any press materials highlighting
this uncomfortable bench.

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:42.750
Excuse me, the "leaning bar."

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:46.260
That’s because it makes for an uncomfortable
discussion about who we design public space

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.519
for — and who gets left out.

